# Rio Grande: traditional capstan shanty, after Whall (1910). Count 6/8 as two beats.
{title: Rio Grande}
{composer: Traditional (sea shanty)}
{key: G}
{tempo: 120}
{time: 6/8}
{x_level: 2}
{x_strum: D - U D - U}

{start_of_grid label="Verse"}
| G . | D . |
| G . | D . |
| G . | C . |
| G . | D G |
{end_of_grid}

{start_of_grid label="Chorus"}
| C . | G . |
| G . | D . |
| G . | C . |
| G . | D G |
{end_of_grid}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Oh, [G]were you ever in [D]Rio Grande?
[G]Way, you [D]Rio!
It's [G]there that the river runs [C]down golden sand,
For we're [G]bound for the [D]Rio [G]Grande.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
And a[C]way, you [G]Rio!
[G]Way, you [D]Rio!
Sing [G]fare ye well, my [C]bonny young girls,
For we're [G]bound for the [D]Rio [G]Grande.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
Good[G]bye, fare ye well, all you [D]ladies of town,
[G]Way, you [D]Rio!
We've [G]left you enough for to [C]buy a silk gown,
For we're [G]bound for the [D]Rio [G]Grande.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{comment: A shortened shanty: two of its traditional verses.}
{x_end: G}
